  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Tranquil weather is expected through the middle\nof the week with climbing temperatures.\n\nCloudy skies linger tonight as the weakening Nor'easter pulls\naway from the coast. Some guidance is hinting at patchy fog\ndeveloping, especially west of I-81 tonight. However, given\nwidespread clouds it will be difficult to get more than patchy\nfog. \n\nFor Tuesday, residual moisture may remain trapped beneath the \nsubsidence inversion with weak gradients on Tuesday, although \ndeeper mixing will likely support more breaks of sun. With \nridging building in at the sfc and aloft and light sfc winds, \npattern will become more conducive for fog development, if skies\nclear enough. This appears more likely to happen late Tue night\ninto Wed morning. \n\nTemperatures will climb further Wednesday and Thursday, with \nmore locations reaching the 80s. Some spots could even reach the\nupper 80s by Thursday. Developing southerly flow will lead to \nmore sunshine. Dry weather is expected both days. With light \nwinds and elevated dew points, some patchy fog could form during\nthe overnight hours.\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front will approach on Friday, leading to\ncooler and potentially unsettled weather for the weekend.\n\nSurface high pressure begins to slide offshore Thursday into Friday. \nAbove-normal temperatures likely persist into Friday afternoon with \ntemperatures approaching the upper-80s as upper-level ridging slowly \nbreaks down ahead of an incoming cold front from the Great Lakes. \nThis front could slowly begin to pass through the Mid-Atlantic by \nFriday evening before stalling just off to the south. \n\nThis front could potentially linger throughout the weekend \nbefore finally pushing offshore towards the start of next week. \nThis will lead to a much cooler weekend overall, along with \nchances for intermittent showers as the front lingers over the \nMid- Atlantic. Rainfall probabilities remain lower (20%-40%) at \nthis time given the weak nature of the front and remaining \nuncertainty in the model guidance. As this front pushes \noffshore, conditions could begin to clear early next week, with \ndaily highs returning to the 60s and lower 70s in the wake of \nthe front.",
